+# Hegel property-based testing report — `goblin`
+
+Target: `goblin` 0.10.6 (m4b/goblin), a cross-platform ELF/PE/Mach-O/archive
+binary parser. Property-based tests added with `hegeltest` 0.28.2.
+
+All new tests live in `tests/hegel_robustness.rs`. There was no pre-existing
+cross-format robustness test file to extend — `tests/{elf,pe,macho,archive}.rs`
+are per-format example tests and `src/lib.rs` holds only unit tests — so these
+cross-cutting properties over `Object::parse` and the four format entry points
+are grouped in one new file.
+
+Toolchain: rustc 1.95.0. `cargo add --dev hegeltest` required
+`--ignore-rust-version` (goblin pins `rust-version = 1.85.0`; hegeltest 0.28.2
+needs 1.86). This affects the dev build only; the library's MSRV is unchanged.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+goblin parses **untrusted** binaries, so the governing contract is
+decoder robustness: no attacker-controlled buffer may cause a panic, over-read,
+hang, or OOM — every entry point must return `Err` on garbage. All properties
+below are oracle-independent (a panic/OOM/over-read/out-of-bounds offset needs
+no external oracle).
+
+| # | Property | Evidence / rationale |
+|---|----------|----------------------|
+| P1 | `Object::parse` on arbitrary bytes never panics; deep iteration of any successful parse is also panic-free | Mirrors `fuzz/fuzz_targets/parse.rs`, extended to drive lazy iterators (ELF notes, PE imports/exports, Mach symbols/relocations/exports/imports, archive members) that only touch the buffer when iterated |
+| P2 | Each direct format entry point (`elf::Elf`, `pe::PE`, `pe::TE`, `pe::Coff`, `mach::Mach`, `archive::Archive`) tolerates arbitrary bytes | `Object::parse` only reaches a parser when the magic matches; calling parsers directly exercises the full parser body on garbage. The fuzz targets only cover `Object::parse` + `elf::Elf::parse` |
+| P3 | Crafted ELF header counts/offsets never panic/OOM | Header fields are attacker-controlled. Takes a real ELF, overwrites exactly `e_phoff`/`e_shoff` (u64, incl. `MAX`) and `e_phnum`/`e_shnum` (u16), preserving magic so the crafted header reaches the table parsers. Directly targets the "huge count / out-of-range offset → gigabyte preallocation or OOB index" class. Source shows the guards (`count > bytes.len()/entsize` before `Vec::with_capacity`) in `elf/{program_header,section_header,sym}.rs`; this property confirms them |
+| P4 | Valid-then-corrupt: N drawn byte flips on a real binary parse gracefully | Skill "valid-then-corrupt" pattern; 14 real fixtures across ELF32/64, PE, TE, Mach-O fat, .NET |
+| P5 | Truncation: every strict prefix of a real binary parses without panic | Skill "truncation" pattern; truncation is where "read a length then read that many bytes" parsers over-read |
+| P6 | `Object::parse` dispatch is consistent with leading magic | Structural invariant: an `Elf` result whose buffer doesn't start with `\x7fELF`, or an `Archive` result without `!<arch>\n`, is a dispatch bug. Run over arbitrary bytes + mutated fixtures |
+| P7 | Parsed archive member offsets/sizes stay within the input buffer | Oracle-independent field-consistency: a member `header_offset`/`offset` past the buffer, or an `extract()` over-read, is a bug. Run over the embedded `crt1a` archive + byte corruptions |
+| P8 | Archive-magic-prefixed arbitrary bytes never panic | Prepending valid `!<arch>\n` drives the member-iteration cursor arithmetic (header parse, decimal size parse, 2-byte realignment, cursor advance) far more often than uniform random bytes |
+
+Generators are deliberately broad: `binary()` up to 4 KiB, full-range `u8`/`u16`/`u64`
+for corruption values and header fields (including `u64::MAX` and buffer-length
+boundaries boosted via `one_of!`/`sampled_from`), and drawn flip-counts/prefix-lengths.
+
+## Test results
+
+All 8 properties **pass**, including an exploratory 10x run (P1/P2/P4/P5/P6/P7/P8
+at 2000 cases, P3 at 3000). No panics, hangs, OOMs, over-reads, or invariant
+violations were observed. The full pre-existing suite (157 unit + integration
+tests) still passes with the dev-dependency added.
+
+Liveness/vacuity verified:
+- P7's in-bounds assertion was inverted (`header_offset > len`) and hegel
+  reported a shrunk counterexample (`n_flips = 0`, member `header_offset = 134`
+  in a 2122-byte buffer) — the guarded Ok branch fires and the assertion is live.
+- The four no-panic properties (P1, P3, P4, P5) were each confirmed to reach
+  their `Ok`/deep-iteration branch (temporary panic hook on the `Ok` arm fired
+  for every one) — they are not passing vacuously.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+**None.** goblin is a heavily-fuzzed, hardened parser (OSS-Fuzz; explicit
+count/offset guards throughout; regression fixtures for issue #450 in
+`archive/mod.rs`). Every allocation from an attacker-controlled count is capped
+against the remaining buffer length *before* `Vec::with_capacity`, and all reads
+go through `scroll`'s bounds-checked `pread`/`gread`, which return `Err` rather
+than panicking. This crate sits in the expected "clean / reference-fuzzed"
+cluster; the properties document and lock in that robustness.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Segment/section offset-within-bounds as a hard invariant (ELF/PE/Mach).**
+  Initially considered a field-consistency invariant like P7. Abandoned for the
+  lazy zero-copy formats: goblin faithfully *reports* header offset/size fields
+  (e.g. ELF `p_offset`/`p_filesz`, PE section RVAs) without requiring them to lie
+  inside the file, because it never dereferences them at parse time. Asserting
+  `offset + size <= len` would be asserting a contract goblin never makes and
+  would produce false positives on legitimate binaries. It is kept only for the
+  archive, where the parser actually advances a cursor by member size and
+  `header_offset`/`offset` are positions the parser itself read (P7).
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Rust reference and SKILL workflow were sufficient; no friction. The
+  `--ignore-rust-version` MSRV gotcha is already documented in the reference and
+  applied cleanly (goblin 1.85 vs hegeltest 1.86).
+- Reinforces an existing memory-note pattern: hardened, reference/OSS-fuzzed
+  decoder crates (here goblin, like earlier fst/etherparse/ruzstd) come back
+  clean. The high-value move on such a crate is not chasing a bug that isn't
+  there but (a) targeting the gaps the crate's own fuzz harness misses — here,
+  deep iteration of parsed **PE/Mach** results and the direct non-`Object`
+  entry points — and (b) pinning the crafted-count/offset OOM guards with an
+  explicit property so a future regression (removing a `count > len/size` cap)
+  would fail a test. The skill could add a short note: "for a crate with a
+  `fuzz/` dir, diff what the fuzz targets exercise against the public API; the
+  untested surface (lazy iterators, sibling entry points) is where a PBT adds
+  signal a green fuzzer doesn't."
